EXT. JUNGLE CLEARING - CRASHED BUSH PLANE - DAY

The fuselage lies like a dead insect, one wing crumpled against a kapok tree. Steam rises from the earth. Insects drone.

CORAZON "CORA" MALABANAN, forties, pilot, sits on the wing with her leg splinted in a map. IFEOMA NNADI, thirties, geologist, kneels at a stream, filling bottles. GRETCHEN HOLM, twenties, intern, paces with a hand over her mouth.

CORA
Gretchen, sit.

GRETCHEN
I can't.

CORA
You can. You're just choosing not to.

IFEOMA
(from the stream)
Water's clean. I tested it.

GRETCHEN
How do you test water?

IFEOMA
I drank a mouthful an hour ago. I'm still here.

CORA
Practical.

IFEOMA
Cora, how long until the beacon pings?

CORA
Two days. Maybe three.

GRETCHEN
Maybe four!

CORA
Maybe four.

Ifeoma crosses and hands Cora a bottle. Cora drinks and passes it to Gretchen, who drinks and hands it back, shaking.

GRETCHEN
It's so quiet.

IFEOMA
It isn't. Listen.

They listen. The drone swells. Under it, something rhythmic. Wood on wood.

CORA
That's a drum.

GRETCHEN
Is that good?

IFEOMA
Possibly.

CORA
Possibly not.

They look at each other. The drumming stops. A hush falls, absolute.

GRETCHEN
(whispering)
What does that mean?

CORA
It means they know we're here.

IFEOMA ^
It means they've been watching us.

Both women speak at once, voices overlapping. Gretchen backs toward the plane.

GRETCHEN
Who are "they"?

The bushes part. A line of figures in painted masks emerges silently. The three women stand shoulder to shoulder.

CORA
(low)
Nobody run.
